<Project>

  <!--
    Shared test settings for every project under test/.

    The single xunit.runner.json beside this file is linked into each test project's output so the
    same parallelization policy applies to all of them rather than to whichever project someone
    remembered. It disables collection parallelism and pins the runner to one thread, so exactly one
    test executes at a time within an assembly.

    Why: the tool's self-validation drives Microsoft Visio and Microsoft PowerPoint through COM
    automation, which is single-instance. Two tests rendering at once tear each other's session down.
    Serializing the suite is slower, and is the accepted cost of a trustworthy one: the product is
    not asked to tolerate contention that only the harness ever created.

    The target frameworks of a multi-target test project are a separate axis that no in-assembly
    setting can reach, because they run as separate processes. See the dotnet test invocation in
    build.ps1.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)xunit.runner.json" Link="xunit.runner.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
